🔍 What I analyzed:
• Customer acquisition and growth
• Trial-to-paid conversion
• Subscription plan adoption
• Plan upgrades and downgrades
• Annual plan adoption
• Customer churn and retention
• Customer subscription journeys
📊 Key findings:
• 1,000 unique customers analyzed
• 90.8% of customers subscribed to a paid plan
• 33.8% of paid customers eventually churned
• 54.6% moved from the trial plan to Basic Monthly
• Customers took an average of 7 days to move from trial to their first paid subscription
• March 2020 recorded the highest customer acquisition, with 94 new customers





📊Actions :

1. Trial-to-paid conversion (7-day average, 54.6% pick Basic Monthly)
Action: Trigger a plan-comparison prompt around day 5, before the average conversion point, so customers see Annual/Pro value before defaulting to Basic Monthly.
Impact: Shifts plan-mix toward higher-value plans without hurting conversion speed.

2. Churn (33.8% of paid customers churned)
Action: Segment churned users by plan and tenure to isolate when they leave, then hand that to product/CS to fix onboarding (if early churn) or retention offers (if late churn).
Impact: Even a 5-10% reduction in churn compounds significantly on recurring revenue.

3. Annual plan adoption
Action: Target engaged monthly users (3+ months tenure) with an annual upgrade incentive.
Impact: Improves retention and predictable revenue (ARR).

4. Downgrades as a leading indicator
Action: Treat downgrade as an early warning signal and trigger automatic retention outreach.
Impact: Proactive save instead of reactive loss.

5. March 2020 acquisition spike (94 new customers)
Action: Dig into acquisition channel data for that month to find the specific driver (campaign, offer, seasonality) and test replicating it.
Impact: Turns a one-time win into a repeatable growth lever.

